#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# block_map

#% text
# `block_map(f, xs, n)` applies `f` to the successive blocks of `n` elements of `xs`; `block_map(f, xs, n, d)` uses blocks with offset `d`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# It is `map(f, partition(xs, n))`; an incomplete last block is dropped.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
block_map(f, range(6), 2)
#% output
#= [f([1, 2]), f([3, 4]), f([5, 6])]

#% cell
block_map(total, range(10), 3)
#% output
#= [6, 15, 24]

#% cell
block_map(f, range(5), 2, 1)
#% output
#= [f([1, 2]), f([2, 3]), f([3, 4]), f([4, 5])]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
block_map(mean, [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11], 2)
#% output
#= [2, 6, 10]

#% cell
block_map(string_join, characters("abcdef"), 3)
#% output
#= ["abc", "def"]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `partition`, `map`, `subsequences`
